{
  "image_name": "a7745c5bf91d3f7b781686f8e7f3cecc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how water harvesting or dehumidification performance is governed by coupled heat and mass transfer across several length scales, from the device level down to molecular sorption sites. It presents a transport pathway linking air-side water movement, transport through a packed sorbent bed, diffusion inside pores, and the final sorption–reaction step on the material surface. At each stage, the diagram highlights that slow transfer can become the bottleneck.\n\nThe middle row emphasizes the main challenges: weak heat and mass transfer outside the sorbent, poor transport among particles, limited movement inside pores, and surface chemistry that is either too strong or too weak for effective uptake and release. These limitations are illustrated with moisture, heat flow, and concentration gradients.\n\nThe bottom row shows corresponding opportunities for improvement. Better interface and component design can enhance exchange at the solid–gas and solid–solid boundaries. More ordered particle arrangements create clearer transport channels. Tailored pore structures improve internal diffusion, and optimized surface functional groups tune sorption behavior. The overall message is that high-performing sorbents require coordinated design of device architecture, particle packing, pore structure, and molecular surface chemistry rather than focusing on only one scale.",
